.TH PINENTRY-FLDESKTOP 1 "November 2026" "fldesktop 0.1.4" "fldesktop"
.SH NAME
pinentry-fldesktop \- GnuPG passphrase prompt for fldesktop
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBpinentry-fldesktop\fR [\fB\-\-display\fR \fIDISPLAY\fR] [\fIpinentry options\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBpinentry-fldesktop\fR asks for passphrases and PINs for \fBgpg-agent\fR(1)
and confirms its questions, in an FLTK dialog in the desktop's colors. It
speaks the Assuan protocol on standard input and output, as every pinentry
does, and shows the quality of a new passphrase, asks for it twice when
gpg-agent wants that, and closes after the agent's timeout.
.PP
When gpg-agent allows it (\fBallow-external-password-cache\fR, the default)
the dialog offers to save the passphrase in the password manager (the Secret
Service: GNOME Keyring or KeePassXC, in the same format as
pinentry-gnome3); the next time it is taken from there without asking. A
passphrase that turned out wrong is forgotten.
.PP
Without a graphical session (a text console, SSH) it runs
\fBpinentry-curses\fR or \fBpinentry-tty\fR instead.
.SH SETUP
Settings > Default Applications chooses it for GnuPG, or in
\fI~/.gnupg/gpg-agent.conf\fR:
.PP
.nf
pinentry-program /usr/bin/pinentry-fldesktop
.fi
.PP
then \fBgpgconf \-\-reload gpg-agent\fR. On Debian the package also offers it as
the \fBpinentry\fR and \fBpinentry-x11\fR alternative.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBgpg-agent\fR(1), \fBfl-keyring\fR(1)
